 in biomedicine</article-title><source>J. Biomed. Info</source><volume>34</volume><fpage>285</fpage><lpage>98</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b36-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sujansky</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Altman</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Towards a standard query model for sharing decision-support applications</article-title><source>Proc 18th Annual Symposium on Computer Applications in Medical Care</source><fpage>325</fpage><lpage>31</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b37-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Swamidass</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Bruand</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Kernels for small molecules and the prediction of mutagenicity, toxicity, and anti-cancer activity</article-title><source>Proc. of the 13th Intl. Conf. on Intelligent Systems for Molecular Biology. Bioinformatics</source><volume>21</volume><issue>Suppl 1</issue><fpage>i359</fpage><lpage>68</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b38-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tsou</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Sun</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>A Biological Data Warehousing System for Identifying Transcriptional Regulatory Sites from Gene Expressions of Microarray Data</article-title><source>IEEE Trans. Info. Tech. Biomed</source>in press.</citation></ref><ref id="b39-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vogelstein</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Lane</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Levine</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Surfing the p53 network</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>408</volume><fpage>307</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11099028</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b40-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Voisard</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>J&#x000fc;rgens</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Geospatial Information Extraction: Querying or Quarrying?</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Goodchild</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Egenhofer</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Fegeas</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Kottman</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><source>Interoperating Geographic Information Systems</source><edition>1st ed</edition><publisher-loc>Dordrecht</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Kluwer Academic</publisher-name><fpage>165</fpage><lpage>79</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b41-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ullman</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>Information Integration Using Logical Views</article-title><source>Proc. 6th Int. Conf. Database Theory. Lecture Notes in Computer Science (LNCS)</source><volume>1186</volume><fpage>19</fpage><lpage>40</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b42-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wallace-Brodeur</surname><given-names>RR</given-names></name><name><surname>Lowe</surname><given-names>SW</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Clinical implications of p53 mutations</article-title><source>Cell. Mol. Life Sci</source><volume>55</volume><fpage>64</fpage><lpage>75</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10065152</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b43-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Rastinejad</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>El-Deiry</surname><given-names>WS</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Restoring p53-dependent tumor suppression</article-title><source>Cancer Biol. Ther</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>S55</fpage><lpage>63</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14508081</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b44-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiderhold</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><article-title>Mediators in the Architecture of Future Information Systems</article-title><source>IEEE Comp</source><volume>25</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>38</fpage><lpage>49</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b45-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Widom</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Research Problems in Data Warehousing</article-title><source>Proc 4th Int Conf Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM)</source><fpage>25</fpage><lpage>30</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b46-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Widom</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1996</year><article-title>Integrating Heterogeneous Databases: Lazy or Eager?</article-title><source>ACM Comp Surv</source><fpage>28</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="b47-cin-02-277"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhou</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Hull</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>King</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Data Integration and Warehousing Using H2O&#x0201d;</article-title><source>IEEE Data Engineering Bulletin, Special Issue on Materialized Views and Data Warehousing</source><volume>18</volume><fpage>29</fpage><lpage>40</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-cin-02-277" position="float"><label>Figure 1.</label><caption><p><bold>CRDB global database schema.</bold> The schema follows a design pattern with &#x0201c;Condition&#x0201d; tables (Molecules, Mutants, Experiments) related to &#x0201c;Results&#x0201d; tables (DockingResults and AssayResults). PK denotes a primary key.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN-02-277-g001"/></fig><fig id="f2-cin-02-277" position="float"><label>Figure 2.</label><caption><p><bold>System architecture and the hybrid strategy to data integration.</bold> Docking and small molecule data use the mediation approach, while the functional and structural assay data use the data warehousing approach. The CRDB is both a mediator and a data warehouse. &#x0201c;Mutants&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;Molecular&#x0201d; are data marts of the warehouse. The ODBC drivers are wrappers in the mediation approach. Dashed lines indicate integration planned in the future.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CIN-02-277-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-cin-02-277" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>CRDB design principles.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>&#x02022; Changes to data</bold><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mediation approach should be used for data that changes often. Data warehousing approach should be used for data that changes periodically.</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>&#x02022; Size of the data</bold><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mediation approach should be used for larger data. Data warehousing approach should be used for smaller data.</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>&#x02022; Availability of sources</bold><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mediation approach should be used for sources that are always available. Data warehousing approach should be used for sources that are often unavailable.</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>&#x02022; Required query processing time</bold><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mediation approach should be used for data with flexible timing constraints. Data warehousing approach should be used for data with stringent timing constraints.</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>&#x02022; Predictability of queries</bold><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Predictable queries on data that does not change often should be written in advance with the results stored in the warehouse. In addition, knowledge of the queries to be performed on the system should be taken into consideration during global schema design.</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-cin-02-277" position="float"><label>Table 2.</label><caption><p><bold>CRDB design method. A.</bold>Data sources were classified based on their characteristics. <bold>B.</bold> Each characteristic was converted to the data integration approach that best implements it. <bold>C.</bold> Each approach was assigned a numerical value with warehouse = 1 and mediation = 0, and a design score was calculated for each data source by summing across the characteristics.</p></caption><table frame="below" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" colspan="6" rowspan="1">A<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Data 